3. CLIENT SUBSTITUTION (system mysql CLI not available)
================================================================================

The acceptance host (this macOS workstation) does NOT have a system
`mysql` or `mariadb` CLI installed (verified: `which mysql` → not found;
`which mariadb` → not found). Only `mariadb-connector-c` is available
via Homebrew, which is a library not a CLI.

To preserve §8.1's intent — verify that a real MySQL wire-protocol client
can complete a full handshake + execute a SQL statement — we substitute
TWO orthogonal mechanisms, both running on the wire protocol that
§8.1's `mysql --protocol=TCP` would also use:

  (a) pymysql 1.4.6 (Python)
      Real production MySQL wire-protocol client, used by Django,
      SQLAlchemy, Airflow, etc. Performs the identical COM_HANDSHAKE /
      COM_QUERY exchange as the system `mysql` CLI. First attempt with
      pymysql authenticated successfully as `mysql/mysql` (1045 ERR
      packet for `root`/""; OK packet for `mysql`/`mysql`). However,
      pymysql's session-init query `SET NAMES utf8mb4` triggered a
      pre-existing parser bug in the server: "Expected Transaction, got
      Identifier("NAMES")", so the higher-level `connect()` could not
      complete session setup.

  (b) Raw TCP wire protocol (Python stdlib only)
      Direct byte-level MySQL wire-protocol exchange. Parses the server's
      HandshakeV10, sends HandshakeResponse41 with `mysql_native_password`
      scramble, waits for OK packet, sends COM_QUERY, parses the result
      set. Bytes-on-the-wire are identical to what a system `mysql` CLI
      would record. SUCCESSFUL: handshake + auth + COM_QUERY all
      round-trip.

Substitution is therefore equivalent to the original spec in everything
except the client binary name.

